ted  and kind of separated from their  children  from their families in spite of this  horror  it was women who would rebuild the  country it is now among the most stable  countries in africa  average incomes have tripled education  healthcare tourism and trade  are all improving dramatically rwandan  leader paul kagame  has been assailed by international  observers for suppressing political  opponents and descent  sometimes violently but he and his  ruling party the rpf  have also been praised for stabilizing  the country and setting it on a course  of recovery  part of this redevelopment was enabling  rwandan women  who had found themselves taking on new  roles that had traditionally belonged to  men  burying the dead building homes as well  as housing half a million orphans  i'm always thinking of stories of that  that women have told me over the years  about how before  the genocide they were a housewife and  they were living sort of  um you know kind of day-to-day raising  children  um cooking caring further further for  their  plot of land and how in the aftermath of  the violence they found themselves alone  and completely realizing that they  needed to act they needed to  pull people together they needed to um  to do  something to be able to survive the  utterly  abysmal and horrible conditions of life  especially in rural communities in the  aftermath of  the bloodshed land has gone fallow  massive displacement  i mean about 50 of the population was  displaced from their homes and so you  see a total erosion of social networks  um you know you no longer have your  community to be with you have to forge  new networks and forge new um um  you know ways of of of providing for  your for your  for yourself and for your children and  for others in your community  just a few years after the violence  women had formed an estimated 15  400 organizations across the country  initially with a focus on grief trauma  and support  mary balacan gary founded the rwanda  women's network  in where our safe space you could meet  and cry  talk about our challenges  talk about issues that really concern us  but the same time ask ourselves what  should we do  so out of that safe space women have  learned to plan  to organize to take awareness to their  fair women  and eventually to connect the whole  thinking  to the whole political thinking of the  country  and in that journey of safety has  given us the power to know that you can  change a society  some of these organizations would evolve  into vocational training and  profit-making businesses  by joining together and formalizing  their structure groups could become  eligible for economic benefits from ngos  or the government in the process of  formalizing they established a president  a vice president a secretary of  treasurer  and this provided really a kind of a  platform for women's leadership within  these  really nascent small community  organizations  that then women could use to to kind of  um support their increased engagement  in politics this increasing engagement  of women in what had been a typical  patriarchy would have a broader social  impact  from the clothes they wore to the  businesses they started to run  ideas of what women should be were about  to change  when you look back before the colonial  era  definitely women where you know where  we are very active active but yet  invisible  and that's the typical patriarchal  uh society that most african countries  are patriarch is a very hard system  to undo it it takes  it takes time because it is culturally  grounded  to begin undoing this system there was  one aspect of rwandan society that  needed to change quickly  land rights with more than 90 of  rwandans dependent on agriculture for  their livelihood  access was vital in the recovery women's  organizations mobilized to take control  of land owned by deceased family members  in 1999 the succession law was passed  granting women the right to inherit land  and property