ther hand  this authoritarian state that has  completely uh jeopardized its  population's well-being  by shutting down dissent by um by  conducting widespread extrajudicial  killings  these two stories are mutually  compatible  they are both true and i think the you  know the reality is that the rwanda's  developmental progress has been made  possible by its authoritarian  nature right rwanda's success in terms  of  not having another outbreak of violence  has been  possible because of its massively robust  military and security apparatus  some of the gains women have made are  undermined by the authoritarian nature  of the state  disproportionate numbers of wealthy  foreign-born tutsi women sit at the top  of the table  which means new forms of inequality are  developing in rwanda  impacting the incredible progress women  made in the immediate aftermath of the  violence  rwanda leads the world when it comes to  the number of women in power  but it's only one part of the bigger  picture of equality  for me the the real the real lesson of  rwanda is that  getting women into politics is only one  of many  things we must do to actually  you know increase women's power and more  importantly dismantle forms of  oppression against women  if the level of women in government is  not matched by a corresponding kind of  strong women's movement  rooted in civil society then the women  in government  are almost always going to be tokenized  that sort of corresponding  need for women in government and kind of  the the restructuring  of the economy of kind of the kind of  patriarchal  roots of the family and of society um  and  and of of changing kind of um the the  the systems of governance as well  like that's the way that we build a more  gender equal  future this process we are going through  as a country i call it every birth  i must say that rwanda when you compare  it to other african countries  we are definitely fortunate because we  have a system  a system that responds 